cture map between them, except the quotient maps forced by inclusion of faces." Hmm, maybe "pieces" here means... it says "no two distinct pieces admit a structure map between them" — existence of ANY structure map between two distinct pieces (except face-forced) is excluded. iB → f exists ⇒ cover not rigid. ⇒ The cofinal family ("standard rigid covers are cofinal" — needed for prop:vanish's setup) is EMPTY. That's an internal contradiction in def:square. — Unless "structure map" means a map in the family-as-a-cover-of-T sense... a map between pieces over T: iB and f are both over T; the map iB → f over P... over T requires T-compatibility: T → f and T → iB and triangle: T → iB → f = T → f ✓ commutes (everything over P). So yes iB → f is a structure map. CONTRADICTION with the cofinality claim ⇒ BLOCKING error in def:square. Deleting iB or f breaks "standard" (must contain both). The deletion procedure itself: "delete any piece with a nonidentity structure map to another surviving piece — a point served by the deleted piece is served via the composition": deleting iB (it maps to f): points served by iB = points factoring P̄ → V tightly... served via composition T → f → V?? only those on f's ray ✗ NOT all tight points. So deletion destroys coverage. def:square is BROKEN as stated. The intended notion was presumably "rigid among folds" (no two folds with a map) which is automatic... and CFA-CFA maps are quotients (necessary for the sieve bookkeeping) and fold-iB maps unavoidable. The Čech combinatorics honestly doesn't even need rigidity. Blocking but easily repaired: drop the rigidity requirement entirely and prove the cofinal family = standard covers (normal form + named pieces) directly.

Also def:square's "then intersect objectwise with the probe family ... of cor:probecovers (possibly doubled to two rays; the intersection is a cover, and all named pieces occur as their own intersections)" — "intersect objectwise": for covers R, S of T: {U ⊗_T V} is a cover (in a Grothendieck topology with fiber products — the "intersection" of covers is a cover by pullback-stability + transitivity ✓); "named pieces occur as their own intersections" since each named piece N ∈ probe-family intersected with some R-piece? N ⊗ U isn't N... "as their own intersections" FALSE literally: f ⊗ f' (both probe, distinct rays) = dead — the named fold f appears as f ⊗ something? f ⊗ U for U ∈ R with kernel J and map... hmm: is f ⊗_T U ≅ some named piece? Not in general. The claim "all named pieces occur" needs: the intersection cover refines... it contains pieces OVER each named piece? Actually: R ∧ S := {U_i ⊗ V_j}: does it refine the probe family? There's a map U_i ⊗ V_j → V_j ✓ so the intersection refines S. Does it contain the named pieces as pieces? Not literally. For the Čech bookkeeping they want covers CONTAINING iB, all CFA pieces, dead piece, two folds AS MEMBERS. Via "intersect with probe family" you get a refinement where every piece maps to a probe piece — but membership of the probes: you'd need pieces with maps FROM the probes — refinement direction: R' refines R means every R'-piece maps to an R-piece (R' below R). To have the probes as MEMBERS of R' while R' refines the original R: need R' = probes ∪ {refined pieces}: is the union a cover? A cover's superset is a cover ✓!! So R' := {all named probes} ∪ (R ∧ probe-cover): covers ✓ contains named pieces ✓. So the cofinality construction is fixable (union, not "occur as their own intersections"), but as written it's wrong/sloppy. And with union-covers, the nerve has arbitrary stuff — fine for the sieve argument? The sieve argument needs the nerve to contain specific pieces with specific kernel behaviors — yes the n